= Molecular genetics
{wiki}

= Plasmid
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <plasmid> is an extrachromosomal <DNA> <replicon>: genetic material maintained by <DNA replication> separately from the host's main <chromosomes>. Separate copying does not mean independence of the host's <enzymes> or resources. Many <plasmids> are circular, but naturally occurring linear <plasmids> also exist.<Conservation of plasmid maintenance functions between linear and circular plasmids in Borrelia burgdorferi by Philip E. Stewart and others>[Stewart, Chaconas and Rosa (2003), abstract opening sentence: natural linear and circular plasmids]{ref}

Engineered <plasmids> can carry <genes> for <genetic transformation> and <functional complementation>. Typical laboratory <plasmids> combine an <origin of replication>, a <selectable marker> and a <plasmid insert>. These are engineering roles, not requirements that every natural <plasmid> carry an experimentally chosen <selectable marker> or <plasmid insert>.<Molecular Biology Reference by Addgene>[Plasmid Elements, Figure 2 and table: origin, antibiotic-resistance gene, insert and promoter; description applies to engineered vectors]{ref}

The useful separation is between keeping a <DNA> fragment, finding its carriers and testing its function. <Molecular cloning> uses propagation to recover more copies for study. <Plasmid restriction mapping> tests a proposed arrangement, while <DNA sequencing> examines its letters. A successful <genetic transformation> or a <biological colony> showing <antibiotic resistance> answers neither question completely. The <yeast cross-species complementation experiment> goes a step further by separating <plasmid> selection from a test of <functional complementation>.

= Plasmid feature map
{parent=Plasmid}

A <plasmid feature map> records the positions of specified <DNA> features. Construct an original circular example of total length $L=6000$ <base pairs>. Coordinates run clockwise from a chosen zero, and $0$ and $6000$ label the same boundary. Positions here denote boundaries between <base pairs>, so the interval from 1000 to 2500 contains $2500-1000=1500$ <base pairs>. This convention avoids counting both endpoints as additional material.

\Image[plasmid-circular-vector-feature-map.png]
{title=An original 6000-<base-pair> <plasmid feature map>: copying, selection and a chosen <plasmid insert> occupy distinct regions}
{description=Hypothetical engineered circular <DNA>, with clockwise boundary coordinates. Blue marks an <origin of replication> at 200–700; green marks a <selectable marker> at 3200–4000; orange marks the <plasmid insert> at 1000–2500. Abstract <restriction enzymes> A and B cut at the labelled boundaries: A at 1000; B at 2500 and 4500. Their recognition <nucleotide sequences> are unspecified. Neither the feature intervals nor the total length describe a real commercial construct. The ring records position, not the physical shape or three-dimensional conformation of <DNA>.}

The <origin of replication> is assigned to 200–700, the <selectable marker> to 3200–4000 and the <plasmid insert> to 1000–2500. Other positions are unspecified <DNA>, not empty gaps. Their lengths account for the entire circle:
$$
500+800+1500+3200=6000.
$$
The first three terms are the marked regions; the last is all remaining material. Choosing a coordinate zero does not add a physical break. Rotating every coordinate by the same amount changes the drawing's labels without changing any distance around the <plasmid>.

A <gene promoter> must function in the recipient for the intended <gene expression>; copying a <plasmid> alone does not establish <gene expression>.<Molecular Biology Reference by Addgene>[Plasmid Elements, Promoter Region row: recipient-dependent transcriptional machinery]{ref} The map therefore supports three separate questions: can the carrier persist, can carriers be recovered, and can the selected <DNA> perform the proposed task? The <galactose induction> comparison illustrates the third question in an actual <yeast> experiment.

= Plasmid insert
{parent=Plasmid}

A <plasmid insert> is the <DNA> fragment added to a carrier <plasmid> during construction. In the <plasmid feature map>, its stipulated boundaries are 1000 and 2500, giving 1500 <base pairs>. The label identifies a construction role; it does not establish <gene expression> or <functional complementation>.

= Plasmid restriction mapping
{parent=Plasmid}

<Plasmid restriction mapping> compares fragment lengths after a <restriction digest> with the pattern predicted by specified cut positions. Use the original 6000-<base-pair> <plasmid feature map>. Abstract <restriction enzyme> A cuts once, at 1000. Abstract <restriction enzyme> B cuts twice, at 2500 and 4500. Assume complete cutting of identical circular copies at exactly those sites, with no loss of <DNA>. These assumptions define a length-accounting exercise; the labels A and B do not stand for particular commercial <enzymes>.

|| Complete <restriction digest>
|| Cut coordinates
|| Linear fragment lengths in <base pairs>

| A
| 1000
| 6000

| B
| 2500, 4500
| 2000, 4000

| A and B
| 1000, 2500, 4500
| 1500, 2000, 2500

For the combined <restriction digest>, successive clockwise distances are
$$
2500-1000=1500,\qquad 4500-2500=2000,
$$
and the final fragment crosses the chosen zero:
$$
(6000-4500)+1000=2500.
$$
Their sum is $1500+2000+2500=6000$. The single A cut opens the circle into one full-length linear fragment. It does not produce pieces of lengths 1000 and 5000, because coordinate zero was never an end.

\Image[plasmid-restriction-digest-selection-logic.png]
{title=<Plasmid restriction mapping> counts physical ends; <plasmid> selection and confirmation answer different questions}
{description=Original length-accounting and inference schematic for the same 6000-<base-pair> construction. The upper strips compare complete A-and-B cutting of the circle with cutting of a separate linear <DNA> substrate that already has physical ends at 0 and 6000. The lower branches show that the <selectable marker> can permit recovery of both an intended construct and an empty carrier. No <biological colony> counts, experimental efficiencies, measured <gel bands> or historical data are depicted.}

For contrast, stipulate a different starting substrate: a linear 6000-<base-pair> <DNA> fragment with physical ends at 0 and 6000 and the same three internal cut coordinates. Its complete <restriction digest> gives
$$
1000,\quad1500,\quad2000,\quad1500
$$
<base pairs>, summing again to 6000. There are four fragments, including two of equal length. Even an ideal length-resolving comparison would show only three distinct sizes if both 1500-<base-pair> fragments occupy the same <gel band>. Fragment number and visible <gel band> number need not agree.

The general counting argument is short. For $k\geq1$ distinct complete cuts of a circle, each fragment runs from one cut to the next, so there are $k$ fragments. For a linear substrate with $k$ distinct internal cuts, its two pre-existing ends give $k+1$ intervals. Summing successive distances counts every original <base pair> once in either case. The rule assumes internal double-strand cuts and does not treat an uncut circle as a zero-length product.

In practice, compare released linear fragments with an appropriate <DNA ladder> using <agarose gel electrophoresis>. The original uncut circular <DNA> need not migrate like a linear fragment of equal length; the existing <agarose gel electrophoresis> discussion explains that shape-dependent limitation. Partial cutting also changes the expected pattern. In this example, a circular copy cut at 1000 and 2500 but not 4500 yields lengths 1500 and 4500. A 4500-<base-pair> product would therefore be compatible with that missed cut rather than uniquely identifying a different construction.

Matching lengths constrain an arrangement without determining every <nucleotide sequence>. For example, any change of letters preserving all three cut positions leaves this predicted pattern unchanged. Even the order of fragments can be ambiguous when only their unordered lengths are observed. <DNA sequencing> across both joins and through the <plasmid insert> tests additional claims that this length model cannot decide.

= 1973 plasmid construction experiment
{parent=Plasmid}

In his account of the 1973 work, <Stanley Norman Cohen> describes purified R6-5 <plasmid> <DNA> cut with <EcoRI>, reassociated and joined by <DNA ligase>. The mixture entered <calcium chloride>-treated <Escherichia coli>; plates containing <antibiotics> selected <biological colonies> showing <antibiotic resistance>. The recovered pSC102 carried three R6-5 fragments and <kanamycin> <antibiotic resistance>. The team then joined material from pSC102 to the carrier pSC101 providing <tetracycline> <antibiotic resistance>, recovering pSC105 with both forms of <antibiotic resistance>.<DNA cloning: A personal view after 40 years by Stanley Norman Cohen>[Cohen (2013), The Hawaii Meeting and the Initial DNA Cloning Experiments, Figure 2 and Figure 3 caption; pSC102, pSC101 and pSC105 identities]{ref}

Stained <agarose gel electrophoresis>, gradient <centrifugation> and <electron microscope> comparisons characterized recovered <plasmids>. Parental and mixture lanes in Figure 3 supplied fragment comparisons; pSC101 alone gave one <EcoRI> fragment, while pSC105 combined its carrier fragment with the resistance-bearing fragment. This supported joined, propagating genetic material beyond selection alone. The comparisons did not determine every <nucleotide sequence> or establish a universal construction efficiency.<DNA cloning: A personal view after 40 years by Stanley Norman Cohen>[Cohen (2013), paragraphs describing the two laboratories' analyses and Figure 3, top and bottom: parental, mixed and recovered DNA comparisons]{ref}

The interesting inference combines two kinds of evidence: a recoverable <phenotype> identifies descendants worth examining, and the extracted <DNA> tests a structural explanation. The fragment comparisons are molecular <scientific controls>; they answer a different objection from a control for background growth. The hypothetical diagrams here illustrate that logic without reproducing the historical fragment lengths or <biological colony> counts.

= Genetic transformation
{parent=Molecular genetics}

<Genetic transformation> introduces <DNA> into recipient <biological cells>. It can change their <genotype> and sometimes their <phenotype>; the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment> separates detecting introduction of a <plasmid> from detecting its functional effect.

= Plasmid selection and confirmation
{parent=Genetic transformation}

<Plasmid selection and confirmation> separate recovering candidates from establishing what they contain. In an original idealized comparison, stipulate a susceptible recipient and a <selectable marker> that permits growth under the chosen condition. The intended 6000-<base-pair> construction and an empty carrier retaining that marker can both yield <biological colonies>. If removal of the 1500-<base-pair> <plasmid insert> followed by <DNA ligation> joins its two boundaries without losing other material, the empty carrier is $6000-1500=4500$ <base pairs>. Selection for their shared <selectable marker> cannot distinguish these candidates.

Use separate <scientific controls> for separate uncertainties. A recipient-only comparison tests background growth under selection. A known intact <plasmid> tests whether recipient preparation, <genetic transformation> and recovery can yield selected carriers. A carrier-only construction comparison tests recovery in the absence of added <plasmid insert>. Growth on supporting <cell culture medium> tests viability under that supporting condition. These are analytical comparisons for the constructed example, not a list claimed to have been performed in the <1973 plasmid construction experiment>.

Recover candidate <DNA>, compare a complete <restriction digest> with the stipulated pattern, and inspect relevant joins by <DNA sequencing>. Each step narrows the claim: selection supports a growth <phenotype>; length comparison supports size and some structural features; <DNA sequencing> tests the letters sampled. Even a confirmed <nucleotide sequence> does not alone show the intended biological function. That requires an appropriate readout such as <functional complementation>, with its own <scientific controls> and recipient <genetic background>.

= Replica plating
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<Replica plating> transfers samples from corresponding <biological colonies> or patches to several growth conditions while preserving their spatial arrangement. A master plate makes it possible to relate growth or failure at one location to the same source population on another plate.

The master preserves the source <biological colonies>; each replica tests a sample of their <biological cells>. A fabric carrier takes one spatial impression from the master and deposits samples onto fresh plates. Different <cell culture medium> formulations can then reveal different growth <phenotypes> at corresponding sites. The carrier copies locations, not a guaranteed number of <biological cells>. <Replica plating spatial correspondence> therefore lets an investigator connect a readout to a recoverable source without treating every empty replica site as a missing master <biological colony>.

\Image[replica-plating-velveteen-spatial-transfer.png]
{title=<Replica plating>: a fabric carrier copies locations while the master preserves the source}
{description=Original apparatus and transfer schematic with invented <biological colony> positions. The left panel separates the inverted plate, fabric pile, retaining hoop and support for clarity. The right panel shows aligned plate views after incubation; the fabric holds an imprint rather than growing the pictured <biological colonies>. Arrows show sample transfer. The dotted return arrow shows using a location to recover material from the unexposed master, not transferring material back from a challenged replica. Sizes and growth outcomes are schematic.}

The useful trick is that a destructive or growth-restricting test can interrogate one sample while another remains available. In <indirect selection of bacterial mutants>, the replica supplies information about which source to recover. This also makes a failure-to-grow <phenotype> accessible: a missing replica <biological colony> cannot be picked, but its source may still be present on the master. Matching positions is the bridge between testing and retrieval.

= Replica plating spatial correspondence
{parent=Replica plating}

<Replica plating spatial correspondence> is the preserved association between a master site and its sampled sites on replicas. Label a master site by an <ordered pair> $(r,c)$, specifying its row and column. Keeping the same orientation and viewing convention makes that label refer to the same source on every plate. The <biological colonies> need not have identical outlines, because the transferred samples can contain different numbers of <biological cells>.

A simple orientation check shows why a circular outline is insufficient. In a constructed four-by-four layout, a half-turn sends $(r,c)$ to $(5-r,5-c)$. Thus B2, or $(2,2)$, would be confused with C3, or $(3,3)$. Applying the half-turn twice returns the original <ordered pair>, but applying it once generally selects the wrong source. An asymmetric orientation mark fixes that ambiguity. Two contact transfers also preserve the handedness when all plates are viewed from the same side: any reflection in going from plate to fabric is undone in going from fabric to plate. A fabric-side view must not be silently substituted for a plate-side view.

= Lederberg replica plating experiment
{c}
{parent=Replica plating}

<Joshua Lederberg> and <Esther Lederberg> reported <replica plating> in 1952. They <autoclave>-sterilized velveteen and secured it pile-up on a wood or cork cylinder with a metal hoop. Inverted <agar> plates touched the fabric lightly. Pencil marks or pin indentations fixed orientation. Complete-medium and minimal-medium replicas compared growth of <biological colonies>.<Replica plating and indirect selection of bacterial mutants by Joshua Lederberg and Esther Lederberg>[pp. 399–402, Methods: Replica plating; Figure 1 caption; Results: plate orientation]{ref}

<Escherichia coli> W-1 grew on plain <agar>; serial T-1 <bacteriophage> replicas showed resistant <biological colonies> at matching sites. Corresponding unexposed master regions seeded repeated broth-and-plain-<agar> enrichment, yielding resistant isolates. Stray-<bacteriophage> checks were negative; resistance persisted without challenge. This supported preexisting heritable resistance. The authors noted variable transfer and moisture-dependent resolution.<Replica plating and indirect selection of bacterial mutants by Joshua Lederberg and Esther Lederberg>[pp. 401–403: transfer variability, Clonal occurrence of phage resistant mutants, Indirect selection and stray-phage checks; p. 404, Discussion]{ref}

The <Luria-Delbrück experiment> uses variation between independently grown <cell cultures> to investigate the timing of resistant <mutations>. The <Lederberg replica plating experiment> adds a spatial route to a recoverable source: the tested samples identify an unexposed lineage. These are complementary arguments about <mutation> and <natural selection>. Growth readouts establish a tested <phenotype>; identifying the responsible <nucleotide sequence> requires other evidence, such as <DNA sequencing>.

= Replica plating controls
{parent=Replica plating}

<Replica plating controls> distinguish a growth <phenotype> from failure of the copying process. A replica on a <cell culture medium> that supports the starting <biological colonies> is a touch-transfer <scientific control>: growth at a site demonstrates that viable <biological cells> reached that control site. If a source is visible on the master but absent on both control and restrictive replicas, the restrictive readout is unresolved. If it grows on the control but fails on the restrictive replica, the difference is a candidate condition-dependent <phenotype>.

The touch-transfer <scientific control> still cannot guarantee transfer to every other replica. A known growing comparison on the restrictive <cell culture medium>, repeat sampling, and retesting a recovered source address different possible failures. Likewise, an orientation mark checks which source a site represents; it cannot check whether any <biological cells> arrived. Gentle, aligned contact helps preserve the map. In Investigations in Molecular Cell Biology by <Clare M. O’Connor>, section 12.6: Exercise 2 - Replica plating and complementation, the instructions align master and carrier marks and call for light, even contact.<Investigations in Molecular Cell Biology by Clare M. O’Connor/12.6: Exercise 2 - Replica plating and complementation>[Investigations in Molecular Cell Biology by Clare M. O’Connor; Introductory light-touch instruction and steps 1–2: orientation marks, aligned inversion and gentle even contact]{ref}

For <indirect selection of bacterial mutants>, keep the recovery route on the unexposed master. Returning a carrier that has touched a challenged replica to that master would introduce a possible exposure route. The original experiment's stray-<bacteriophage> check addresses contamination of the retained lineage, while the orientation and touch-transfer <scientific controls> address spatial identity and successful sampling. These questions must be checked separately.

= Indirect selection of bacterial mutants
{parent=Replica plating}

<Indirect selection of bacterial mutants> recovers a source using a related sample's tested <phenotype>, rather than directly challenging the retained lineage. A positive example uses a surviving replica <biological colony> to locate its unexposed source. A negative example uses failure to grow in one condition to locate a source that does grow elsewhere. The second is particularly useful for finding <auxotrophy>: dependence on an externally supplied nutrient.

Construct a four-by-four teaching layout with rows A–D and columns 1–4. The master has 13 <biological colonies>; A4, D1 and D4 are empty. A growth-supporting control replica grows at every occupied master site except B2 and C3. A replica lacking an assumed nutrient X grows at those same control sites except A2 and C1. All other requirements are stipulated equal. These are invented readouts, not observations from the <Lederberg replica plating experiment>.

\Image[replica-plating-colony-map-retrieval.png]
{title=An original <replica plating> map separates retrieval candidates, unresolved transfer and absent sources}
{description=Constructed four-by-four master, control and nutrient-X-omission readouts, viewed in the same orientation. Blue discs mean growth. Orange rings identify A2 and C1 for recovery from the master. Red crosses mark B2 and C3, which lack growth even on the control; gray dashes mark positions empty on the master. The lower comparison distinguishes these three explanations of an empty restrictive-replica site. No measured data or published plate illustration is reproduced.}

Let $M$, $C$ and $S$ be the <finite sets> of sites with growth on the master, control and X-omission replica respectively. In this construction $S\subseteq C\subseteq M$, with <cardinalities>
$$
|M|=13,\qquad |C|=11,\qquad |S|=9.
$$
The <set difference>
$$
C\setminus S=\{\mathrm{A2},\mathrm{C1}\}
$$
contains the two condition-dependent candidates. Pick from A2 and C1 on the master, then compare fresh samples of each recovered source with and without X. Restoration of growth when X is supplied supports an X-requirement <phenotype>, subject to the <scientific controls>; growth alone does not identify a particular <gene> or <mutation>.

By contrast,
$$
M\setminus C=\{\mathrm{B2},\mathrm{C3}\}
$$
is unresolved sampling or viability in this first comparison. Neither site should be counted as an X-requirement candidate. The three sites outside $M$ have no master source to recover at all. The short logical argument is membership testing: a candidate must belong to $C$, establishing control growth, and not belong to $S$, establishing the contrasting readout. Being outside $S$ alone supplies only the second condition. Among the seven empty X-omission sites, two are candidates, two are unresolved and three have no master source. The beauty of the method is that a shared location turns this logical distinction into a practical retrieval route.

= Genome
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <genome> is the complete set of genetic information under discussion. For a cellular <organism> it is encoded in <DNA>, including <genes> and other sequences. Specify whether a comparison concerns a nuclear <genome>, a <mitochondrion>'s <genome> or all cellular genetic material: the <cell nucleus> does not contain every <DNA> sequence in a typical <eukaryote>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Genome[NHGRI: genome].

= Genome mapping
{parent=Genome}

Locating <genetic markers> or other features within a <genome>. Physical positions use <DNA> sequence distances, while linkage maps use <genetic recombination> frequencies.

= DNA sequencing
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<DNA sequencing> determines the order of <nucleotide> bases in <DNA>. Sequence data can test molecular rearrangements, as in the <functional plastid-to-nucleus gene transfer experiment>, or support <phylogenetics> after a suitable <sequence alignment>.

An ordered <nucleotide sequence> answers a different question from fragment size. The eight-<nucleotide> strands ACGTTAGC and AACCGGTT have equal lengths and identical counts of <adenine>, <cytosine>, <guanine> and <thymine>, yet different orders. A length-compatible <gel band> therefore cannot identify either strand by itself. <Sanger sequencing> makes length informative by deliberately coupling each stopping position to the identity of its last added <nucleotide>.

That coupling is the beautiful experimental idea: convert an invisible order into a collection of ordered endpoints. <Sanger ladder reading> recovers the newly synthesized strand; <base pairing> and the <reverse complement> then connect that readout to the original template. The distinction also matters when checking a <mutation>: a reliable local <nucleotide sequence> supports a particular molecular change, while its effect on a <phenotype> requires additional evidence.

= Sanger sequencing
{c}
{parent=DNA sequencing}
{wiki}

= Chain-termination sequencing
{synonym}

= Dideoxy sequencing
{synonym}

<Sanger sequencing> determines a <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> by extending a common <sequencing primer> and occasionally incorporating a <dideoxynucleoside triphosphate>. A normal <deoxynucleoside triphosphate> already has <hydrogen> in place of a $2'$ <hydroxy group>; it retains the $3'$ <hydroxy group>. A <dideoxynucleoside triphosphate> lacks that $3'$ <hydroxy group> too. It can join the growing strand because the preceding <nucleotide> supplies the reacting $3'$ <hydroxy group>. Once incorporated, its own $3'$ end cannot form the next <phosphodiester bond>. Thus the blocked step is the next addition, not attachment of the terminator itself.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Isolating, Cloning, and Sequencing DNA>[Alberts and others, Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002), Isolating, Cloning, and Sequencing DNA, Figure 8-36 and accompanying dideoxy-method explanation]{ref}

\Image[sanger-dntp-ddntp-chain-extension.png]
{title=A <dNTP> retains a $3'$ <hydroxy group>; a <ddNTP> permits incorporation but blocks the next extension}
{description=Original chemical-feature and chain-extension schematic. Both substrates have $2'$-H; only the <dNTP> has $3'$-OH. Boxes omit most <chemical atoms> and do not specify stereochemistry. The lower rows show incorporated units, which no longer carry the substrate's complete triphosphate group. The previous strand end attaches either substrate; the difference appears at the new terminal $3'$ end. This is a conceptual drawing, not molecular imaging or experimental data.}

In the historical four-reaction format, each tube receives copies of the same template, the same <sequencing primer>, a <DNA polymerase> and all four ordinary <deoxynucleoside triphosphates>. The tubes differ in their terminating substrate: ddATP, ddCTP, ddGTP or ddTTP. In the A reaction, ordinary dATP can allow copying to continue past one A position, whereas incorporation of ddATP stops another copy there. Competition produces a family of endpoints, rather than every strand stopping at the first A. Adjusting substrate proportions makes later endpoints detectable. A lane identifies the terminating <nucleobase> in the new strand, not the complementary template letter.<Genomes, 2nd edition by Terence A. Brown/Sequencing Genomes>[T. A. Brown, Genomes, 2nd edition (2002), section 6.1.1, Chain termination sequencing in outline]{ref}

The readout requires <gel electrophoresis> capable of separating single-stranded <DNA> fragments differing by one <nucleotide>. Historical <denaturing polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis> suppressed strand folding and resolved these adjacent lengths; radioactive labels were detected by <autoradiography>. An ordinary <agarose gel electrophoresis> comparison with a 100-<base pair> <DNA ladder> does not supply that resolution. Four separate reaction lanes carry the identities in this format.<Genomes, 2nd edition by Terence A. Brown/Sequencing Genomes>[T. A. Brown, Genomes, 2nd edition (2002), section 6.1.1, Figure 6.1, Figure 6.2 and Technical Note 6.1: single-nucleotide resolution and autoradiographic readout]{ref}

= Sanger ladder reading
{c}
{parent=Sanger sequencing}

Consider an original teaching template whose next eight positions, encountered after the <sequencing primer>, are
$$
3'\text{-}\mathrm{TGCAATCG}\text{-}5'.
$$
The <sequencing primer> binds an upstream region omitted from this display; the template also continues beyond the eight positions shown. <DNA polymerase> encounters the displayed template from left to right. Applying <base pairing> at each successive position gives the extension
$$
5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTTAGC}\text{-}3'.
$$
These eight letters are added material, not the entire primer-containing <DNA> <molecule>.

\Image[sanger-four-lane-ladder-reading.png]
{title=Read an original four-lane <Sanger sequencing> ladder from the shortest fragment upward to recover $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTTAGC}\text{-}3'$}
{description=Invented ideal endpoints: A at extension positions 1 and 6; C at 2 and 8; G at 3 and 7; T at 4 and 5. Every fragment contains the same <sequencing primer> of length $p$; the shortest drawn band is $p+1$ <nucleotides>, not a one-<nucleotide> whole strand. The gel is a schematic of <denaturing polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis>; band spacing, widths and darkness are illustrative, with no measured migration, abundance or experimental photograph. The right panel shows the complete added prefix represented by each endpoint.}

Here is the full endpoint accounting. A terminal letter denotes an incorporated terminating <nucleotide>; all preceding letters of that extension were supplied by ordinary <deoxynucleoside triphosphates>.

|| Added position $n$
|| Reaction lane
|| Added prefix, $5'$ to $3'$
|| Whole-fragment length

| 1
| A
| A
| $p+1$

| 2
| C
| AC
| $p+2$

| 3
| G
| ACG
| $p+3$

| 4
| T
| ACGT
| $p+4$

| 5
| T
| ACGTT
| $p+5$

| 6
| A
| ACGTTA
| $p+6$

| 7
| G
| ACGTTAG
| $p+7$

| 8
| C
| ACGTTAGC
| $p+8$

The shortest fragments migrate farthest, placing position 1 at the bottom. Reading upward, the lane labels spell A, C, G, T, T, A, G, C: the new strand in its $5'$-to-$3'$ direction. The adjacent T bands at positions 4 and 5 encode two separate additions. A single unresolved T patch would leave that count uncertain. In the A lane, the position-6 fragment passed position 1 using ordinary dATP and stopped at position 6 using ddATP; it does not skip the intervening <nucleotides>.

The common <sequencing primer> is an offset. For primer length $p$, endpoint $n$ has length $p+n$ <nucleotides>. With an illustrative $p=20$, the drawn fragments would contain 21 through 28 <nucleotides>, and adjacent endpoints still differ by one. Subtracting the common $p$ preserves their order because $(p+n+1)-(p+n)=1$. This supplies a short justification for <Sanger ladder reading>: nested fragments share a start, length orders their endpoints, and lane membership supplies each endpoint's letter. A missing early band cannot be relabeled position 1 without independent evidence about the starting point.

To report the original template region, first complement the readout in aligned orientation:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{new extension:}&\quad 5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTTAGC}\text{-}3',\\
\text{aligned template:}&\quad 3'\text{-}\mathrm{TGCAATCG}\text{-}5'.
\end{aligned}
$$
If the requested answer is that template written $5'$ to $3'$, reverse the aligned template to obtain $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GCTAACGT}\text{-}3'$, the <reverse complement> of the readout. Reversing the readout alone would give CGATTGCA and would be wrong. Complementing without reversing gives the correct template letters in its displayed $3'$-to-$5'$ direction, but not in the usual reporting convention. Specify the strand and its direction before transforming the answer.

= Sanger sequencing controls
{c}
{parent=Sanger sequencing}

The endpoint argument identifies what its <scientific controls> must interrogate. A known-template comparison tests whether preparation, copying and detection can produce an interpretable ladder. A no-template comparison tests background arising without the intended template. Agreement from an independently primed opposite-strand read tests the proposed <nucleotide sequence> through its <reverse complement>. These are distinct analytical checks; none follows merely from seeing a <gel band>.

An ambiguous ladder should retain its ambiguity. More than one band at a nominal position can reflect mixed templates or unwanted stops; a missing band can reflect failed detection rather than an absent <nucleotide>. Four clean lanes are an assumption of the ideal example. They do not establish a unique <genome>, a biological function, or the frequency of a <mutation> in a population. The historical observations below show why a control for copying chemistry matters even before interpreting the letters.

= Sanger-Nicklen-Coulson sequencing experiment
{c}
{parent=Sanger sequencing}

In 1977, <Frederick Sanger>, <S. Nicklen> and <Alan Coulson> tested chain termination on <Phi X 174> <DNA>. They isolated <restriction enzyme> fragments as primers, heated them with template to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for three minutes and annealed at $67^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for thirty minutes. Four extension mixtures used the <Klenow fragment> of <DNA polymerase> and radioactive dATP. Their Figure 1 used ddGTP, ddATP, ddTTP and araCTP, an alternative terminating substrate; Figure 2 used all four dideoxy types. This distinction matters when relating the original experiments to an all-ddNTP teaching model.<DNA sequencing with chain-terminating inhibitors by Frederick Sanger and others>[Sanger, Nicklen and Coulson, DNA sequencing with chain-terminating inhibitors, PNAS 74 (1977), pp. 5463–5465, Sequencing Procedure and Figures 1–2]{ref}

Figure 1 used a 12% acrylamide gel at $40\,\mathrm{mA}$ for fourteen hours, read by <autoradiography>. Omitting the unlabeled dATP chase caused unwanted A stops across samples: a chemistry <scientific control>. Ordered bands supported local <nucleotide sequences>; comparisons could revise provisional assignments. Weak T bands, unresolved long runs and structure-dependent migration limited interpretation. The paper's observations are evidence from particular preparations, not a guarantee that every endpoint is resolved.<DNA sequencing with chain-terminating inhibitors by Frederick Sanger and others>[Sanger, Nicklen and Coulson (1977), p. 5464, chase-omission observation and Figure 1 caption; p. 5466, Results: weak T bands, unresolved runs, sequence comparisons and structure-dependent migration]{ref}

= Sequencing primer
{parent=DNA sequencing}

A short <DNA> strand used to initiate a <DNA sequencing> read.

= Sequence alignment
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <sequence alignment> places <nucleotide sequences> or <protein> sequences in correspondence for comparison. The choice of corresponding positions affects evolutionary inference; matching a short stretch alone does not establish <common descent> of entire <organisms>.

= Polymerase chain reaction
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

= PCR amplification
{c}
{synonym}

The <polymerase chain reaction> amplifies a selected <DNA> region using repeated copying steps. Amplified material can be examined by <DNA sequencing>; appropriate template controls help distinguish a new rearrangement from material already present in the starting preparation.

The essential trick is to choose two <PCR primers> on opposite <DNA> strands with their extendible $3'$ ends facing into the same interval. A <DNA polymerase> extends each <PCR primer> $5'\to3'$; the resulting <DNA> can itself become a template. The <amplicon> is therefore selected by two opposed <PCR primers>, rather than by the <DNA polymerase> recognizing a named <gene>. This makes <PCR amplification> useful for inspecting one interval within a much larger <genome>. Here PCR means <polymerase chain reaction>; the statistical abbreviation <PCR> means <principal component regression>.<Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) by Addgene>[Background Information: two primers, template-directed copying and product verification]{ref}

For a deliberately tiny <nucleotide sequence> example, display the upper strand as $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGT}\text{-}\mathrm{GGAATT}\text{-}\mathrm{CAGC}\text{-}3'$. The left <PCR primer> is $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGT}\text{-}3'$ and binds the lower strand. The right <PCR primer>, written in its own $5'$-to-$3'$ direction, is $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GCTG}\text{-}3'$: the <reverse complement> of CAGC. It binds the upper strand and extends leftward in the drawing. With flanks outside this interval, the bounded <amplicon> contains $4+6+4=14$ <base pairs>, including both <PCR primer> sequences. These four-<nucleotide> <PCR primers> illustrate direction and counting only; they are not a proposed experimental design.

\Image[pcr-opposed-primers-strand-cycles.png]
{title=Opposed <PCR primers> define the <amplicon> through successive copying cycles}
{description=Original schematic, not molecular imaging. The upper panel follows separation, binding and $5'$-to-$3'$ extension on the two original <DNA> strands. The lower panel follows one selected lineage: cycle 1 makes a strand with one primer-defined end; cycle 2 copies it to make the first strand bounded at both ends; cycle 3 copies that bounded strand to make a bounded duplex. Other products are omitted. Primer colors identify the two <PCR primers>, not different <nucleobases>; extensions continue beyond the second site when the available template extends farther.}

A useful way to understand the <polymerase chain reaction> is to separate recognition from construction. <Base pairing> positions a <PCR primer>; a <DNA polymerase> then joins new <nucleotides> onto that existing strand. The <PCR primer> becomes part of the copied <DNA>, rather than merely marking a place for an independent strand to start. A <PCR thermal cycle> repeatedly exposes templates, positions starting ends and permits construction. This makes <DNA> copying possible outside <biological cells>, with the chosen <PCR primers> specifying which interval is repeatedly accessible.

= PCR primer
{c}
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}

A <PCR primer> is a short <DNA> strand that supplies a paired $3'$ end for a <DNA polymerase>. Its <nucleotide sequence> determines where <base pairing> can initiate copying. Matching a <PCR primer> at one site is insufficient: both <PCR primers> must support opposed extension across a copyable interval for sustained selective <PCR amplification>.

= Amplicon
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}
{wiki}

An <amplicon> is a <nucleic acid> product of amplification. In ordinary <PCR amplification> without added primer tails, its boundaries eventually correspond to the outer ends of the two <PCR primer> binding sites. First-cycle extensions on long starting templates can run beyond the opposite site; exact-length single strands appear in cycle 2 and exact-length duplex products in cycle 3 under complete extension. This explains why the bounded product becomes dominant even though the initial extensions need not have its length.

= PCR thermal cycle
{c}
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}

A <PCR thermal cycle> separates three jobs. During <PCR denaturation>, heating separates the paired <DNA> strands without intentionally cutting their <phosphodiester bonds>. During <PCR annealing>, cooling permits <base pairing> between <PCR primers> and templates. During <PCR extension>, the chosen interval supports <DNA polymerase> synthesis from the paired $3'$ ends. Each new <nucleotide> is supplied as a <deoxynucleoside triphosphate>; its incorporation lengthens the strand. The <DNA polymerase> is a <catalyst>, while these <nucleotides> are consumed substrates.<Principles of PCR by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[Principles of PCR, accompanying description: denaturation, annealing, primer-dependent extension and dNTP incorporation]{ref}

<Taq polymerase> withstands repeated high-<temperature> strand separation better than a heat-sensitive <DNA polymerase>. Its extension activity still depends on <temperature>, <buffer solution> and <magnesium> ions. Heat resistance does not mean constant activity at every stage: the separation <temperature> serves the <DNA>, whereas the extension <temperature> serves the copying <enzyme>. As one institutional example, Addgene's Basic PCR Program specifies $94^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ separation, $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ primer binding and $72^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ extension. These are example settings, not universal properties of all <PCR primers> or <DNA polymerases>.<Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) by Addgene>[Basic PCR Program, steps 1–6; Materials List: Taq buffer with magnesium chloride]{ref}

\Image[pcr-thermal-cycle-stage-chart.png]
{title=The three jobs in a <PCR thermal cycle>: separate, pair, extend}
{description=Original schematic of three stipulated cycles with $94$, $55$ and $72^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ holds lasting $20$, $25$ and $45\,\mathrm{s}$ respectively. Horizontal widths represent these assumed hold durations. Vertical transitions omit heating and cooling time; this is neither a measured sample-temperature trace nor a validated experimental program. Initial separation, final extension and storage holds are omitted.}

For timing arithmetic alone, those stipulated holds give $20+25+45=90\,\mathrm{s}$ per <PCR thermal cycle>, and three cycles give $270\,\mathrm{s}$. Adding an assumed $90\,\mathrm{s}$ initial separation and $180\,\mathrm{s}$ final extension gives $540\,\mathrm{s}=9\,\mathrm{min}$ of holds. A real <thermal cycler> also takes time to move between <temperatures>. The schedule tells us when conditions favour <PCR denaturation>, <PCR annealing> or <PCR extension>; it does not measure the fraction of <DNA> copied.

An actual institutional protocol makes the distinction concrete. New England Biolabs specifies mixing templates and <PCR primers> with its OneTaq master mix in a thin-walled tube; a quick spin collects liquid at the bottom. Its $25\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ preparation uses $12.5\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ of the twice-concentrated mix and $0.5\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ of each $10\,\mathrm{\mu M}$ <PCR primer> stock, with <water> completing the volume. Tubes enter a <thermal cycler> preheated to $94^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$; mineral oil is specified if there is no heated lid. The program gives thirty cycles of $94^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ separation, $45$–$68^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ binding and $68^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ extension, with extension time scaled to product length. The binding setting must be selected for the <PCR primers>; the range is not a continuously swept setting.<Protocol for OneTaq 2X Master Mix with Standard Buffer by New England Biolabs>[Protocol, Reaction setup steps 1–2, 25 microlitre table and Thermocycling conditions; General Guidelines, Annealing and Extension]{ref}

The dilution can be checked independently: each <PCR primer> has final <concentration> $10\times0.5/25=0.2\,\mathrm{\mu M}$, while the mix is diluted twofold. For an endpoint readout, Addgene specifies examining a product aliquot by <gel electrophoresis>.<Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) by Addgene>[Procedure, Primer Design and PCR step 13: run 2 microlitres on a gel]{ref} Pair that size comparison with the existing <PCR controls>: a known template and a no-template tube test different explanations of success or failure. Those comparisons are an analytical addition, not controls documented in the OneTaq schedule. An expected-sized band supports production of a length-compatible <amplicon>; it does not establish its <nucleotide sequence>, ideal <PCR efficiency> or successful separation during every cycle. Neither protocol supplies a measured temperature or yield curve for the schematic above.

= PCR denaturation
{c}
{parent=PCR thermal cycle}

<PCR denaturation> is the high-<temperature> stage that separates a <DNA> duplex into single-stranded templates for the next copying step. It disrupts the pairing and stacking arrangement of <nucleobases>; it does not require cutting the <phosphodiester bonds> along either strand. The distinction matters: breaking the backbone would damage the ordered <nucleotide sequence> that is meant to serve as a template.

For an original eight-<base pair> duplex, complete <PCR denaturation> exposes two eight-<nucleotide> strands. It leaves sixteen <nucleotides> altogether: separation has made the templates accessible without yet making more <DNA>. <PCR extension>, rather than heating alone, adds the new material. Already copied duplexes also need to separate at the beginning of subsequent cycles.

Separation is a condition-dependent process. Template composition and the <buffer solution> affect the required <temperature> and duration; heating longer is not automatically better because prolonged heating can reduce <DNA polymerase> activity. Incomplete separation leaves fewer accessible templates even when the <PCR primers> themselves are well chosen.<PCR Cycling Parameters—Six Key Considerations for Success by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[Template DNA denaturation assessment, paragraphs 1 and 3–6; DNA denaturation considerations, paragraph 1]{ref}

= PCR annealing
{c}
{parent=PCR thermal cycle}

<PCR annealing> is association of <PCR primers> with exposed complementary <DNA> regions by <base pairing>. It supplies the paired starting ends required for <PCR extension>. Cooling can permit this association without itself joining new <nucleotides> onto the <PCR primers>. The two templates can also pair with one another again, so exposure after <PCR denaturation> is an opportunity for binding, not a guarantee of productive copying.<Principles of PCR by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[Description, first and final paragraphs: binding to complementary templates precedes primer-dependent copying]{ref}

In the example below, $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AGTC}\text{-}3'$ pairs with the aligned template segment $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TCAG}\text{-}5'$. The opposite end labels show <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>. Pairing the right letters in the wrong directional display does not supply a correctly depicted starting end. These four-<nucleotide> strands are a teaching example, not a laboratory <PCR primer> recommendation.

The binding <temperature> balances enough productive pairing against unwanted binding. Its appropriate value depends on the <PCR primers> and reaction conditions; increasing it can improve <PCR specificity> but can also prevent useful binding. A predicted binding setting therefore needs experimental assessment rather than being inferred from the label PCR.<PCR Cycling Parameters—Six Key Considerations for Success by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[Primer annealing optimization, paragraphs beginning In this step, Note that the calculated, and If nonspecific PCR products]{ref}

= PCR extension
{c}
{parent=PCR thermal cycle}

<PCR extension> is template-directed construction of <DNA> from an annealed <PCR primer> by a <DNA polymerase>. Incoming <deoxynucleoside triphosphates> supply the new <nucleotides>. Each addition lengthens the growing strand at its $3'$ end, so the product grows $5'\to3'$ while the template is read $3'\to5'$. The direction refers to the <nucleic acid> backbone, not to left or right on a page.<Principles of PCR by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[Description, final paragraph: extension of annealed primers by dNTP incorporation]{ref}

\Image[pcr-primer-extension-polarity.png]
{title=<PCR extension> follows the paired $3'$ end on either template orientation}
{description=Original eight-position <DNA> example with separated complementary templates. Dark rails are existing templates; coloured rails are new strands. The blue <PCR primer> grows rightward and the green <PCR primer> grows leftward. Orange marks the material added by <DNA polymerase>; both arrows point from the original paired $3'$ end toward the new $3'$ end. Labels at each end show that both products grow $5'\to3'$. The templates stop at the displayed boundaries for this teaching example; real starting templates can extend farther. Four-<nucleotide> <PCR primers> are deliberately too short to constitute an experimental design.}

For a complete worked copying step, start with template $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TCAGGGAC}\text{-}5'$ and paired <PCR primer> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AGTC}\text{-}3'$. The next four template letters are GGAC, encountered in the template's $3'$-to-$5'$ direction. The <base pairing> rule therefore selects CCTG in order, giving
$$
5'\text{-}\underbrace{\mathrm{AGTC}}_{\text{primer}}\underbrace{\mathrm{CCTG}}_{\text{extension}}\text{-}3'.
$$
The <PCR primer> supplies four <nucleotides>, and <PCR extension> adds four more; this copying event consumes four <deoxynucleoside triphosphates>. On the oppositely oriented template $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AGTCCCTG}\text{-}3'$, the other <PCR primer> is $5'\text{-}\mathrm{CAGG}\text{-}3'$. It pairs at the right and extends leftward. Reading that template from right to left encounters CTGA after the paired region, so addition of GACT gives $5'\text{-}\mathrm{CAGGGACT}\text{-}3'$. Both finished products are the <reverse complements> of their respective templates.

The direction argument is short: the <PCR primer> and its template are <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>; extension takes place at the <PCR primer>'s $3'$ end; hence forward progress along the new strand is backward progress along the template. Reflecting a drawing reverses its arrows on the page without changing either chemical direction. This is why a leftward arrow can represent $5'\to3'$ synthesis.

The time needed depends on how much <DNA> remains to be copied and the chosen <DNA polymerase>, while <temperature> must support that <enzyme>'s activity.<PCR Cycling Parameters—Six Key Considerations for Success by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[Primer extension considerations, paragraphs 1 and 3: enzyme activity, synthesis rate and target length]{ref} Separating <PCR annealing> and <PCR extension> into different holds is convenient, but some compatible conditions allow a combined hold. The three chemical jobs therefore need not always require three distinct <temperatures>.<Principles of PCR by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[Description, paragraphs 2–3: enzyme-dependent extension temperature and two-step cycling]{ref}

= Thermal cycler
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}
{wiki}

A <thermal cycler> changes the <temperature> of reaction tubes through a programmed sequence. A heated lid can reduce evaporation and condensation above the reaction. Instrument timing, sample <temperature> and nominal block <temperature> need not be identical.

= PCR efficiency
{c}
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}
{title2=$E_n$}

For a stipulated population of amplifiable copies, let $N_n$ be the amount after cycle $n$ and define <PCR efficiency> by the fractional new yield
$$
N_{n+1}=N_n(1+E_n),\qquad 0\leq E_n\leq1.
$$
Here $E_n=1$ means one additional copy per existing copy, hence ideal doubling. If every cycle has the same <PCR efficiency> $E$, the <exponential growth> model gives $N_n=N_0(1+E)^n$. Starting with $N_0=100$ copies, twenty ideal cycles give $104\,857\,600$ copies; the stipulated value $E=0.8$ gives about $12\,748\,236$. An efficiency of 80 percent therefore means multiplication by 1.8 each cycle, not retention of 80 percent of the final ideal yield.

\Image[pcr-stipulated-efficiency-cycle-curves.png]
{title=Stipulated <PCR efficiency> models: ideal doubling, slower <exponential growth> and a <PCR plateau>}
{description=Original calculated curves, not laboratory observations or measured <fluorescence>. All begin at $N_0=100$. Blue has $E_n=1$; orange has $E_n=0.8$; green stipulates $E_n=0.9(1-N_n/K)$ with $K=10^8$. The left panel uses a <logarithmic scale> for copy amount; the right shows the assigned cycle efficiencies. Only the green model includes a finite ceiling. The illustrative ceiling and its equation are assumptions, not fitted chemical kinetics or a universal explanation of a plateau.}

In the ideal strand-counting example, all duplex-equivalent material doubles, but exact-length <amplicons> lag initially. Starting from one longer duplex and retaining its two original strands, cycle $n\geq1$ contains $2(2^n-n-1)$ exact-length strands out of $2^{n+1}$ total strands. Thus their fraction is $1-(n+1)/2^n$: zero after cycle 1, one quarter after cycle 2 and one half after cycle 3. This counts strands rather than assuming every strand immediately finds an exact-length partner. The generic yield model above ignores this early distinction.

= PCR plateau
{c}
{parent=PCR efficiency}

A <PCR plateau> is the late phase in which further cycles add little product. Depletion of substrates and accumulation of reaction by-products can reduce <PCR efficiency>. Consequently, similar final <amplicon> amounts need not mean similar initial template amounts. In the stipulated green model, $0<N_n<K$ implies $N_n<N_{n+1}<K$, and growth approaches the imposed ceiling. That illustrates loss of starting-amount information without claiming to identify the limiting chemical process in an actual tube.<PCR Cycling Parameters—Six Key Considerations for Success by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[PCR cycle number determination and Figure 7: lowered efficiency and plateau from reaction-component depletion and by-products]{ref}

= PCR specificity
{c}
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}

<PCR specificity> concerns whether the accumulated <amplicon> comes from the intended interval. Binding <temperature>, <PCR primer> <nucleotide sequences> and their possible alternative binding sites matter. Too permissive binding can support competing products; excessively restrictive binding can prevent the intended product. A band of the expected length is useful evidence, but unrelated <nucleotide sequences> can have the same length. <DNA sequencing> tests identity more directly, subject to mixtures and copying errors.<How to Design a Primer by Addgene>[Primer Design for PCR: specificity, paired melting temperatures and complementary primer sequences]{ref}

= Primer dimer
{parent=PCR specificity}
{wiki}

A <primer dimer> is an amplification artifact in which <PCR primers> provide extendible partners for one another. Its accumulation consumes substrates and may produce a signal even without the intended template. It is not evidence that the intended <amplicon> was present.<Why do I see amplification curves in my NTC samples? by New England Biolabs>[FAQ: primer structures as DNA-polymerase substrates, and carryover PCR-product contamination]{ref}

= PCR controls
{c}
{parent=PCR specificity}

<PCR controls> are <scientific controls> that separate different explanations for a readout. A no-template reaction contains the <PCR primers> and copying reagents but replaces the added template with <water> or sample buffer. Amplification there can indicate carried-over <DNA> or a <primer dimer>; a clean result does not exclude contamination confined to one sample.<How can I distinguish non-template amplification (NTC) from real products? by New England Biolabs>[FAQ: target-free control reactions and differing product sizes or melt profiles]{ref}

Different comparisons answer different questions. A known amplifiable template tests whether the <PCR primers>, <DNA polymerase> and <thermal cycler> can produce the expected <amplicon>. An extraction blank follows sample preparation without adding the sampled material. A separate amplifiable interval in the sample can test whether recovered <DNA> supports copying, although success there does not guarantee success at the target. A length standard calibrates <gel electrophoresis>; it is not a control for the copying reaction. These comparisons are an analytical design extension to the teaching example below, not a claim that its student protocol includes every comparison.

= PV92 teaching experiment
{c}
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}

The DNA Learning Center's <Detection of an Alu Insertion Polymorphism by PCR by DNA Learning Center, Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory> supplies a concrete length-based example. It collects mouthwash <biological cells>, concentrates them with a <microcentrifuge>, heats them by <Chelex extraction> and transfers the <DNA>-containing <supernatant>. Its $25\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ reaction combines $2.5\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ of that preparation with primer mix and a reagent bead in a <thermal cycler>. Thirty cycles use $94$, $68$ and $72^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$, each for thirty seconds. An electrophoresis chamber separates products by <agarose gel electrophoresis> in a 2 percent gel; a <UV transilluminator> reveals stained <DNA>.<Detection of an Alu Insertion Polymorphism by PCR by DNA Learning Center, Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory>[pp. 2–3, Procedures I–III: extraction, 25 microlitre reaction, cycle profile, gel and UV readout]{ref}

Its <PCR primers> flank an insertion site at the <PV92 locus>. The student protocol predicts a 715-<base pair> product with the <Alu element> and a 415-<base pair> product without it. Two bands support a <heterozygous> <genotype>; one corresponding band supports a <homozygous> <genotype> only if amplification and detection of both <alleles> were adequate. These are the protocol's expected readouts, not newly collected observations.<Detection of an Alu Insertion Polymorphism by PCR by DNA Learning Center, Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory>[p. 1, Introduction: primer-flanked insertion and stated 715/415 bp genotype patterns]{ref}

The instructor manual describes extra bands from nonspecific priming and a small diffuse <primer dimer> band. A missing target band therefore cannot on its own distinguish failed extraction, inhibited copying, poor binding or lack of the target. Nor does detection of the <Alu element> at this one <genetic locus> establish a complete <genome>, a unique personal identity or a clinical conclusion. The <PCR controls> help separate these possibilities; <DNA sequencing> can add sequence evidence to the size comparison.<Using an Alu Insertion Polymorphism to Study Human Populations by Dolan DNA Learning Center, Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory>[printed pp. 18 and 29, Results and Discussion and Amplify DNA by PCR: primer dimer and nonspecific priming]{ref}

= Reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}
{wiki}

= RT-PCR
{c}
{synonym}

<RT-PCR> combines <reverse transcription> with <PCR amplification>. The RT names the <RNA>-to-<DNA> step, not real-time observation. <RT-PCR> can have an endpoint readout. A matched preparation omitting <reverse transcriptase> tests whether contaminating <DNA> could account for amplification; it answers a different question from a no-template reaction.<Basic Principles of RT-qPCR by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[One-step vs. Two-step RT-qPCR and Controls for RT-qPCR: minus-reverse-transcription control]{ref}

= Real-time polymerase chain reaction
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}
{wiki}

= Quantitative PCR
{c}
{synonym}

= qPCR
{c}
{synonym}

<Real-time polymerase chain reaction> monitors <fluorescence> during cycling. With validated specificity, calibration and <PCR efficiency>, that record supports estimating initial template amount; this quantitative use is called <qPCR>. Ordinary endpoint <PCR amplification> examines accumulated product afterwards. <qPCR> need not start from <RNA>, and <RT-PCR> need not monitor <fluorescence>. Combining both operations gives <RT-qPCR>.<Real-Time PCR (qPCR) Basics by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[Real-Time PCR Basics: amplification monitoring and quantification]{ref}

= RT-qPCR
{c}
{parent=Real-time polymerase chain reaction}

<RT-qPCR> applies <qPCR> after <reverse transcription>. Differences in <RNA> recovery and conversion into <complementary DNA> can affect its inferred starting amount as well as differences in <PCR efficiency>.

= Polymerase chain reaction history
{parent=Polymerase chain reaction}

The 1993 Nobel chemistry announcement credits <Kary Mullis> with inventing the <polymerase chain reaction> method and states that the technique was first presented in 1985. The reusable-product principle explains its power: each successfully copied interval can help specify the next generation. The award attribution is a historical claim; it does not imply that every improvement in <DNA polymerases>, <thermal cyclers> or <PCR controls> came from one person.<Press release: The 1993 Nobel Prize in Chemistry by Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences>[13 October 1993 announcement, award citation and introductory account of PCR first presented in 1985]{ref}

= PV92 locus
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}

The <PV92 locus> is a <genetic locus> on human chromosome 16 used in the <PV92 teaching experiment> to compare the presence and absence of an <Alu element>.

= Alu element
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

An <Alu element> is a repeated <DNA> element; the <PV92 teaching experiment> examines one particular insertion site rather than amplifying every copy across the <genome>.

= Chelex extraction
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}

<Chelex extraction> releases <DNA> from heated <biological cells> in the presence of a resin that binds metal ions. The <PV92 teaching experiment> transfers the <supernatant>, leaving resin and debris behind.

= Reverse transcription
{parent=Molecular genetics}

<Reverse transcription> makes <DNA> from an <RNA> template using a <reverse transcriptase>. It reverses the template/product relationship of ordinary <transcription>, which makes <RNA> from <DNA>. The resulting <complementary DNA> can supply a template for <PCR amplification>.<Basic Principles of RT-qPCR by Thermo Fisher Scientific>[RNA as the Starting Material and Reverse Transcriptase Enzymes]{ref}

= Reverse transcriptase
{parent=Reverse transcription}
{wiki}

An <enzyme> that can synthesize <DNA> from an <RNA> template during <reverse transcription>.

= Complementary DNA
{c}
{parent=Reverse transcription}
{wiki}

= cDNA
{c}
{synonym}

<Complementary DNA> is <DNA> made from an <RNA> template by <reverse transcription>. When its template is mature <messenger RNA>, its sequence reflects the processed transcript rather than every intervening <intron> in the corresponding <gene>.

= Northern blot
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <Northern blot> separates <RNA> by size and detects selected sequences through hybridization with a probe. Detection of a <messenger RNA> supports <transcription> of a <gene>, but does not alone establish a functional <protein> or its destination.

= DNA replication
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<DNA replication> copies a double-stranded <DNA> <molecule> into two daughter double-stranded <DNA> <molecules>. In <semiconservative replication>, each daughter retains one parental strand and receives one newly synthesized complementary strand. <DNA polymerases> build the new strands from incoming <nucleotides>; they do not make <RNA> or <proteins> in this copying step. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002): DNA replication mechanisms].

For a five-<base pair> example, separate $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGCC}\text{-}3'$ from its partner $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$. <Base pairing> specifies a new TACGG partner for the old ATGCC strand and a new ATGCC partner for the old TACGG strand. With error-free copying, both daughter <DNA> <molecules> have the original <nucleotide sequence>, but different physical strand histories. One initial duplex contains ten <nucleotides>; one complete round produces two duplexes containing twenty <nucleotides>, ten retained and ten newly incorporated.

\Image[dna-replication-semi-conservative.png]
{title=<Semiconservative replication>: follow two original <DNA> strands through two complete copying rounds}
{description=Blue O1 and purple O2 are the original strands; green strands were made in round 1 and orange strands in round 2. Each arrow connects a parent duplex to its daughters. After round 2, two of four duplexes retain an original strand, and the other two retain a round-1 strand. All eight strands have the appropriate complementary <nucleotide sequences>; colors identify strand age, not different <nucleobases>. The flattened duplexes show ancestry and orientation, not a replication fork or molecular shape.}

This is useful for inheritance: both daughter <biological cells> can receive a copy of the <DNA> record. The elegant feature is that <base pairing> lets each strand specify its missing partner. Direction matters too: a replicating <DNA polymerase> reads its template $3'\to5'$ and extends the new strand at its $3'$ end, so synthesis proceeds $5'\to3'$. These are opposite directions on <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>, not two alternative directions of synthesis. The enzyme needs a pre-existing paired strand end to extend; the diagram omits the machinery that starts and coordinates copying. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/[Alberts et al.: template-directed synthesis and the requirement for a primer].

The strand accounting predicts observable differences between copying mechanisms. In 1958, Meselson and Stahl grew <Escherichia coli> with the heavier <nitrogen> <isotope> $^{15}\mathrm{N}$, then supplied $^{14}\mathrm{N}$ and measured extracted <DNA> by density-gradient centrifugation. After one generation their samples contained intermediate-density <DNA>; after two, intermediate-density and light <DNA> occurred in equal amounts. This supported <semiconservative replication> in the tested <Escherichia coli> culture. It was an <isotope> assay of sampled <DNA>, not simultaneous testing of every kind of <DNA> or direct colored-strand photography. See <The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl>.

= Semiconservative replication
{parent=DNA replication}
{wiki}

In <semiconservative replication>, each daughter duplex retains one strand of its immediate parent. Under complete copying without strand loss or exchange, one starting duplex gives $2^n$ duplexes after $n$ rounds, but still only two original strands. For $n\geq1$, the fraction of duplexes containing an original strand is $2/2^n=2^{1-n}$: all after one round, half after two, a quarter after three. The fraction of all strands that are original is instead $2^{-n}$. A strand made in round 1 is parental when round 2 begins.

= DNA polymerase
{c}
{parent=DNA replication}
{wiki}

A replicating <DNA polymerase> is an <enzyme> that extends a paired strand end with <nucleotides> selected against a <DNA> template. New <DNA> grows $5'\to3'$ while the template is read $3'\to5'$. <Base pairing> specifies the partner; the <DNA polymerase> catalyzes its incorporation. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/[Alberts et al.: DNA polymerase].

= Taq polymerase
{c}
{parent=DNA polymerase}
{wiki}

<Taq polymerase> is a heat-resistant <DNA polymerase> used in <PCR amplification>. It requires a paired strand end and <nucleotide> substrates; heat resistance does not confer target recognition.

= Klenow fragment
{c}
{parent=DNA polymerase}
{wiki}

= Deoxynucleoside triphosphate
{parent=DNA replication}

= dNTP
{c}
{synonym}

A <deoxynucleoside triphosphate> is a <nucleotide> containing <deoxyribose> and three <phosphate> groups. <DNA polymerases> use these substrates to extend <DNA>; the four standard substrate types carry <adenine>, <cytosine>, <guanine> or <thymine>.

= DNA replication inhibitor
{c}
{parent=DNA replication}

A <DNA replication inhibitor> interferes with <DNA replication>; the observed effect depends on exposure and assay conditions.

= Hydroxyurea
{parent=DNA replication inhibitor}
{wiki}

<Hydroxyurea> supplies the synthesis-blocking comparison in the experiment under <EdU labeling>.

= Dideoxynucleoside triphosphate
{parent=DNA replication}

= ddNTP
{c}
{synonym}

A <deoxynucleoside triphosphate> analogue with $3'$-H in place of $3'$-OH, used in <Sanger sequencing>.

= Replicon
{parent=DNA replication}

A <replicon> is a <DNA> unit replicated from an <origin of replication>. A <plasmid> can form a <replicon> separate from a host <chromosome>.

= Origin of replication
{parent=DNA replication}

An <origin of replication> is a <DNA> region at which <DNA replication> is initiated. Its function depends on compatible cellular machinery.

= DNA repair
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<DNA repair> addresses damage to <DNA>. Incorporation of a labeled precursor during <DNA repair> need not indicate duplication of the whole <genome> in <S phase>.

= Gene expression
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<Gene expression> uses a <gene>'s information to produce functional <RNA> or a <protein>. <Transcription> makes <RNA> from a <DNA> template; for a <protein>-coding <gene>, <biological translation> then uses <messenger RNA> to specify an <amino acid> chain. Many <genes> instead produce functional <RNA>, including <transfer RNA> and <ribosomal RNA>, without a translated <protein> product. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002): from DNA to RNA].

Follow the chosen <coding strand> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGGCTTAA}\text{-}3'$: <transcription> of its complementary template gives $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGGCUUAA}\text{-}3'$ in <messenger RNA>. In the stated <reading frame>, its three <codons> are AUG, GCU and UAA: <methionine>, <alanine> and a <stop codon>. Thus nine <nucleotides> specify two <amino acid> residues followed by termination, not three residues. This is an illustrative coding segment embedded in suitable expression and initiation context, not a claim that an isolated nine-<nucleotide> <RNA> is sufficient to initiate <biological translation>.

\Image[gene-expression-transcription-translation.png]
{title=<Gene expression>: oriented <DNA> template, <messenger RNA> <codons> and the resulting <amino acid> order}
{description=The <coding strand> matches the displayed <messenger RNA> after T is replaced by U; <RNA polymerase> uses the opposite, complementary <DNA> strand. The <ribosome> reads AUG, GCU and UAA from $5'$ to $3'$. AUG and GCU specify <methionine> and <alanine>; UAA signals termination without supplying an <amino acid>. The chain is shown from its <N-terminus> to its <C-terminus>. The labels summarize successive operations, not simultaneous binding of all depicted components; flanking signals and machinery are omitted.}

The distinction between copying and use explains how <biological cells> can preserve the same <DNA> yet produce different products. <DNA replication> duplicates the record; <gene expression> selects and uses parts of it. <Transcription> does not use all <DNA> at once, and <gene expression> can be controlled at several steps, including <transcription>, <RNA> processing and <biological translation>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-1-regulation-of-gene-expression[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: regulation of gene expression]. The arrows describe template-directed synthesis: the <DNA> does not physically turn into a <protein>, and the <messenger RNA> does not supply the <amino acids> from which that <protein> is assembled.

= Galactose induction
{parent=Gene expression}

In the GAL1 <gene promoter> system used in the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>, the inducing <sugar> galactose promotes <gene expression> from the engineered construct, whereas <glucose> provides a noninducing comparison. The comparison concerns an imposed regulatory system, not the donor <species>’ native regulation. <Pathways Over Time Plasmids Engage Students in Functional Genomics Research by Clare O’Connor>[O’Connor (2018), Plasmid features enable multiple student experiments and Complementation reveals conservation of function: GAL1 promoter]{ref}

= Promoter
{disambiguate=genetics}
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

= Gene promoter
{synonym}

A <gene promoter> is a <DNA> region involved in initiating <transcription>. Moving a coding sequence into a nuclear <genome> does not guarantee that its original <gene promoter> can drive nuclear <gene expression>.

= Transcription factor
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

A <transcription factor> is a <protein> that regulates <transcription>. Combinations of <transcription factors> help select <gene expression> patterns during <cell differentiation>. <Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others>[Anatomy and Physiology 2e, §3.6, Differentiation and Figure 3.35: transcription factors]{ref}

= Transcription
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

= Transcription
{synonym}

<Transcription> is synthesis of <RNA> using one <DNA> strand as a template by an <RNA polymerase>. During elongation the <RNA polymerase> reads that template $3'\to5'$ while extending <RNA> $5'\to3'$. Only the selected region is transcribed; other regions need not be active, and the template strand can differ between <genes>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al.: RNA polymerase and selection of transcribed DNA regions].

For the displayed example, align these three <nucleotide sequences> in the same left-to-right positions:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{coding DNA:}\quad&5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGGCTTAA}\text{-}3',\\
\text{template DNA:}\quad&3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACCGAATT}\text{-}5',\\
\text{RNA product:}\quad&5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGGCUUAA}\text{-}3'.
\end{aligned}
$$
The first template triplet TAC selects AUG: T pairs with A, A with U, and C with G. The next CGA selects GCU, and ATT selects UAA. The <coding strand> therefore matches the <RNA> lettering except for T versus U, whereas the template is complementary. Reading that template in its own $5'\to3'$ direction gives $5'\text{-}\mathrm{TTAAGCCAT}\text{-}3'$; orientation cannot be discarded. The <gene expression> diagram keeps all three rows aligned. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-2-prokaryotic-transcription[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: template and coding strands in transcription].

This correspondence makes <nucleotide sequences> interpretable, but replacing T by U is valid only after identifying the <coding strand>. Moreover, <RNA> may be processed after <transcription>, so the initial product and mature <messenger RNA> need not have identical uninterrupted <nucleotide sequences>. <Transcription> also makes functional <RNA> that will not undergo <biological translation>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al.: RNA processing and RNA as the final gene product].

= RNA polymerase
{c}
{parent=Transcription (biology)}
{wiki}

An <RNA polymerase> used in <transcription> is an <enzyme> that synthesizes <RNA> from a <DNA> template. Unlike a replicating <DNA polymerase>, it can start a new strand without a pre-existing paired strand end; initiation still requires the appropriate <DNA> signals and associated machinery. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al.: RNA polymerase and transcription initiation].

= Coding strand
{parent=Transcription (biology)}
{wiki}

For a specified <transcription> unit, the <coding strand> is the <DNA> strand matching the initial <RNA> product in the $5'\to3'$ direction, with T in place of U. The opposite <DNA> strand is the template. The name does not imply that every transcribed <gene> encodes a <protein>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-2-prokaryotic-transcription[Clark, Douglas and Choi: coding and template strands].

= Translation
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

= Biological translation
{synonym}

<Biological translation> uses a <ribosome> to read <messenger RNA> <codons> $5'\to3'$ and assemble the corresponding <amino acid> chain. <Transfer RNA> adaptors match <codons> through <base pairing> and carry the <amino acids>; the <genetic code> supplies the correspondence. The chain grows from the <N-terminus> toward the <C-terminus>, by adding each new residue at the <C-terminus>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: ribosomes, transfer RNA and protein synthesis].

In the <gene expression> diagram, read $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUG\ GCU\ UAA}\text{-}3'$ in the chosen <reading frame>. Under the standard <genetic code>, AUG specifies <methionine> and GCU specifies <alanine>; UAA is a <stop codon>. The result before any subsequent processing is the two-residue chain N–<methionine>–<alanine>–C, followed by termination. The <stop codon> contributes zero <amino acids>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: the standard genetic code]. This example assumes appropriate initiation at the displayed AUG in a suitable context in a <eukaryote>; the diagram displays the coding segment, not a complete independently translatable <messenger RNA>.

Initiation establishes the starting position and <reading frame>, using a <ribosome>, an initiating <transfer RNA> and additional factors. At a standard <stop codon>, a release factor promotes release of the chain rather than delivery of another <amino acid> by <transfer RNA>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis[Clark, Douglas and Choi: initiation and termination of translation]. A short two-residue example illustrates decoding, not the size or folding of a typical functional <protein>.

The useful connection is between two different alphabets: <nucleotides> specify the order of <amino acids> through the <genetic code>. <Base pairing> helps select <transfer RNA>, while the <ribosome> catalyzes joining the delivered <amino acids>. The <messenger RNA> remains a template rather than being consumed as the chain's building material. Separating template, adaptor and product makes clear why <transcription> and <biological translation> are distinct mechanisms.

= Genetic code
{parent=Translation (biology)}
{wiki}

The <genetic code> assigns <RNA> <codons> to <amino acids> or termination signals. Four possible <nucleobases> at each of three positions give $4^3=64$ <codons>. In the standard <genetic code>, 61 specify <amino acids> and three are <stop codons>; several <codons> can specify the same <amino acid>. Variants exist, so state which <genetic code> is being used. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi: genetic code assignments and variants].

= Codon
{parent=Genetic code}

A <codon> is a three-<nucleotide> unit read in a specified <reading frame> during <biological translation>. For example, GCU specifies <alanine> in the standard <genetic code>. Its letters are read $5'\to3'$.

For an original example, choose the <messenger RNA> segment $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUG\ GCU\ UUC\ UGA}\text{-}3'$ and stipulate initiation at the displayed AUG in a suitable <eukaryotic> context. Under the standard <genetic code>, its first three <codons> specify <methionine>, <alanine> and <phenylalanine>; the fourth is a <stop codon>. The resulting <peptide> before processing is N–<methionine>–<alanine>–<phenylalanine>–C. Twelve <nucleotides> therefore specify three <amino acid residues> and termination. The displayed segment omits the surrounding initiation signals; its <nucleotide sequence> alone is not a complete expression system.

A <codon> belongs to an oriented <nucleotide sequence>: GCU and UCG are different ordered triplets. Four possible <nucleobases>, <adenine>, <cytosine>, <guanine> and <uracil>, allow $4\times4\times4=64$ triplets because each position can be chosen independently when enumerating possible sequences. This is a counting argument, not a claim that <nucleobases> occur independently in real <RNA>. Sixty-one triplets are sense <codons>, meaning <amino acid> assignments, and UAA, UAG and UGA are <stop codons> in the standard <genetic code>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§15.1, The Genetic Code, Figure 15.4 and following paragraph: triplets and standard assignments]{ref}

\Image[codon-reading-frame-sequence.png]
{title=<Codons> connect an oriented <RNA> segment to an <amino acid> order}
{description=Original constructed <nucleotide sequence> under the standard <genetic code>. Only the first row assumes initiation at AUG; the other two <reading frames> merely regroup the same letters. The bottom row inserts one C after AUG and retains that starting position. Grey trailing letters do not form complete <codons> within the displayed segment.}

The useful unit is the ordered triplet together with its <reading frame> and <genetic code>, rather than three letters viewed in isolation. That combination lets <DNA sequencing> data support predictions about <protein> sequences after identifying the relevant <messenger RNA>. The <Nirenberg and Matthaei experiment> illustrates how the correspondence became experimentally accessible.

= Stop codon
{parent=Codon}
{wiki}

In the standard <genetic code>, UAA, UAG and UGA are <stop codons>: they signal termination of <biological translation> rather than an <amino acid> assignment. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi: stop codons].

A <stop codon> supplies no additional <amino acid residue>. In the constructed AUG GCU UUC UGA example, replacing UGA by UAA preserves termination after the same three <amino acid residues>. Replacing UUC by UAA instead terminates after <methionine> and <alanine>. These are consequences of the stated <genetic code>, not measurements of expression efficiency.

At a standard <stop codon>, a <release factor> promotes release of the <polypeptide> from the <ribosome>. Ordinary delivery of an <amino acid> by <transfer RNA> does not supply a “stop <amino acid>”.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§15.5, Translation, Elongation, and Termination, paragraph beginning “Termination of translation”: stop codons and release factors]{ref}

= Release factor
{parent=Stop codon}

A <release factor> is a <protein> involved in terminating <biological translation> and releasing the completed <polypeptide> at a <stop codon>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§15.5, Translation, Elongation, and Termination, paragraph beginning “Termination of translation”]{ref}

= Reading frame
{parent=Genetic code}
{wiki}

A <reading frame> specifies how consecutive <nucleotides> are grouped into <codons>. Starting at position 1 of AUGGCUUAA gives AUG / GCU / UAA; starting at position 2 gives UGG / CUU with AA left over. The letters are unchanged but the groups differ. A possible grouping alone does not establish an actual <biological translation> initiation site.

For the twelve-<nucleotide> example, number positions from the $5'$ end. The three possible forward <reading frames> start at positions 1, 2 and 3:
$$
\begin{aligned}
1:&\quad\mathrm{AUG\;GCU\;UUC\;UGA},\\
2:&\quad\mathrm{UGG\;CUU\;UCU}\quad(\mathrm{GA}),\\
3:&\quad\mathrm{GGC\;UUU\;CUG}\quad(\mathrm{A}).
\end{aligned}
$$
Parentheses enclose incomplete trailing groups. The second and third rows do not assert initiation of <biological translation> in a <biological cell>. A chosen start fixes which positions belong together; the <ribosome> then advances through consecutive <codons> rather than searching each three-letter window independently.

= Frameshift mutation
{parent=Reading frame}
{wiki}

A <frameshift mutation> changes downstream grouping into <codons> through insertion or deletion of a number of <nucleotides> not divisible by three. In the constructed <messenger RNA> example, insert C immediately after AUG while retaining the same initiation position: $\mathrm{AUG\;CGC\;UUU\;CUG}\;(\mathrm{A})$. The first <codon> is preserved, but the original downstream groups GCU, UUC and UGA disappear. The displayed fragment now has no complete standard <stop codon>; what happens later depends on its continuation.

The short proof is positional: every downstream <nucleotide> moves by $k$ positions after an insertion of length $k$. Boundaries recur every three positions, so they retain the original phase exactly when $k$ is divisible by three. An insertion of three <nucleotides> can still change the <peptide>, but it does not shift the downstream <reading frame>. This distinction helps interpret <genetic variants> without predicting a particular <phenotype> from grouping alone.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§15.1, The Genetic Code, Figure 15.5: nucleotide deletion and shifted grouping]{ref}

= Degeneracy of the genetic code
{parent=Genetic code}

<Degeneracy of the genetic code> means that distinct <codons> can specify the same <amino acid>. For example, UUU and UUC both specify <phenylalanine> in the standard <genetic code>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§15.1, Figure 15.4 and paragraph defining degeneracy]{ref} In the original AUG GCU UUC UGA example, changing UUC to UUU preserves the predicted <amino acid> order. This illustrates redundancy of assignment, not equality of every possible effect on <gene expression>.

<Degeneracy of the genetic code> differs from ambiguity: multiple inputs share one output, whereas an ambiguous assignment would give a single <codon> more than one meaning in the same specified decoding context. The standard <genetic code> table has one listed assignment per triplet. Alternative <genetic codes>, including those used in some <mitochondria>, and engineered decoding contexts must be specified separately; the standard table is not a universal statement about all <organelles> or synthetic systems.

= Nirenberg and Matthaei experiment
{c}
{parent=Genetic code}
{wiki}

In 1961, <Marshall Warren Nirenberg> and <J. Heinrich Matthaei> ground washed <Escherichia coli> and used <centrifugation> to prepare a <cell-free extract>. Reactions at $35^\circ\mathrm{C}$ combined <ribosomes>, soluble components, <ATP> regeneration and <phenylalanine> carrying a $^{14}\mathrm{C}$ <isotope> label, with or without <polyuridylic acid>, an <RNA> containing only U. After incubation, <trichloroacetic acid> <precipitation (chemistry)>, washing and counting <radioactive decay> detected label in insoluble material. Added <polyuridylic acid> strongly increased incorporation; removing <ribosomes> or <ATP> regeneration suppressed it. Product characterization supported a <phenylalanine> <polypeptide>.<The dependence of cell-free protein synthesis in E. coli upon naturally occurring or synthetic polyribonucleotides by Marshall Warren Nirenberg and J. Heinrich Matthaei>[pp. 1589–1590, Methods and Materials; p. 1592, Table 1 assay; pp. 1595–1600, Tables 6–9 and Figure 6]{ref}

\Image[cell-free-polyuridylic-acid-decoding.png]
{title=<Nirenberg and Matthaei experiment>: preparation, comparison and readout}
{description=Original schematic; readout summarizes qualitative published observations.}

The inference connects an imposed <RNA> template to a specific <amino acid> product. Yet repeated U alone cannot distinguish one-, two- or three-letter coding units: every possible group still contains only U. Neither triplet length nor the complete <genetic code> follows.<The dependence of cell-free protein synthesis in E. coli upon naturally occurring or synthetic polyribonucleotides by Marshall Warren Nirenberg and J. Heinrich Matthaei>[p. 1601, Discussion and Note added in proof: coding-unit length not established]{ref}

= Cell-free translation
{parent=Translation (biology)}

<Cell-free translation> uses isolated <ribosomes> and soluble components to make <polypeptide chains> from <messenger RNA> outside an intact <biological cell>.

= Non-coding DNA
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

= Auxotrophy
{parent=Molecular genetics}

<Auxotrophy> is a <phenotype> in which growth requires an externally supplied substance that a comparison <biological strain> can make for itself. Growth on supplemented <cell culture medium> formulations can reveal the requirement without identifying the responsible <gene>.

= DNA ligation
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}

<DNA ligation> joins <DNA> ends covalently through <DNA ligase>. <Base pairing> of complementary ends can align fragments before joining.

= Molecular cloning
{parent=Molecular genetics}

<Molecular cloning> propagates a chosen <DNA> fragment using a carrier such as a <plasmid> in suitable recipient <biological cells>.
